{
  "dom_id": "step-q216459",
  "name": "Question 19",
  "problem": "$\\,$ ${f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary and sufficient for the above statement to be true?\n\n- $f$ is a differentiable function\n- $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$\n- $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C,$ for any $C\\in R$",
  "worked_solution": "Given that the equivalence $P⇔Q$ is true, we have that $P$ is a *necessary and sufficient condition* for $Q.$  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary and sufficient* for the given one. In other words,\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,⇒\\,\\text{Condition}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,⇐\\,\\text{Condition}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the solution to the differential equation ${f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C\\in R$ is the arbitrary constant.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,⇍\\,(f\\text{ is a differentiable function})\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the function $f(x)={e}^{x}$ is a differentiable function but ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne {sec}^{2}⁡x.$\n\n- Condition II is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,⇏\\,(f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, for the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1,$ the statement ${f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ is true but $f(x)\\ne \\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1.$\n\n- Condition III is both necessary and sufficient: The statement ${f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ is true *if and only if* $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C,$ for any $C\\in R.$\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,⇒\\,(f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,⇐\\,(f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,⇔\\,(f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
